    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          This is the one sealing problem where waiting is sometimes exactly the wrong response. Some causes resolve with warmth and time; one of the most common cannot resolve at all, and identifying which you have decides everything.
        </p>

        <div id="how-long" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Is Too Long?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Set expectations before diagnosing, because countertop sealers cure slowly by design and a lot of reported problems are impatience.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Touch Dry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hard</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Full Chemical Cure</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating silane/siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-4 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-72 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-6 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-14 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Epoxy, two-part</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-12 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-72 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-30 days</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wax</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buff immediately</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">N/A</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            So an epoxy that is still slightly soft at 24 hours is behaving normally. An epoxy that is liquid, sticky or fingerprinting at 72 hours in a warm room is not, and that is the point to start diagnosing rather than waiting further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distinguish soft from tacky. A film that is firm but takes a light fingernail impression is curing and needs time. A film that transfers to your finger, stays wet-looking, or remains gummy days later has a problem that time will not necessarily solve.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single most important diagnostic question for a two-part product is whether the tackiness is uniform or patchy. Uniform tackiness across the entire surface points strongly at the mix. Patchy tackiness in specific areas points at thickness, contamination or moisture underneath.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FIRST QUESTION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Is it a two-part product, and was it measured by weight or volume as specified?</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Mis-measured epoxy is the most common cause of a countertop sealer that never hardens, and unlike cold or moisture it cannot be rescued by waiting. If the ratio was wrong, the chemistry cannot complete, and the only route forward is removal.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="mix-ratio" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Mix Ratio and Mixing: The Cause That Never Recovers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two-part epoxies and some polyurethanes cure by a chemical reaction between resin and hardener in a fixed stoichiometric ratio. Get that ratio wrong and there is surplus of one component with nothing to react with, and it stays in the film permanently as an uncured, sticky fraction.
          </p>

          <p>
            The failure is not proportional to the error in a forgiving way. A ten percent deviation can leave a film that never fully hardens. And it does not matter which direction the error goes — extra hardener is as bad as extra resin, which surprises people who assume more hardener means a harder cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the way the manufacturer specifies. Some products are measured by weight, some by volume, and the two ratios are different because the components have different densities. Measuring a by-weight product by volume produces exactly this failure, and it is a very easy mistake to make with a measuring jug on the bench.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mixing thoroughly matters as much as the ratio. Scrape the sides and the bottom of the container repeatedly, because unmixed resin clinging to the walls goes onto the countertop as a patch that will not cure. Mix for the full time stated, which is usually longer than feels necessary, then transfer to a second clean container and mix again — the double-pot method that professionals use precisely to eliminate unmixed material from the container walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Respect the pot life. Mixed epoxy begins reacting immediately and generates its own heat, which accelerates the reaction further. Beyond its pot life it thickens and will not flow or level properly, and applying it in that state produces a poor film even if the ratio was perfect. Mix in batches you can apply within the stated window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diagnostically: uniform tackiness across the whole countertop, on a two-part product, in normal conditions, is a ratio or mixing failure until proven otherwise. Patchy tackiness with sticky spots in specific places is more likely unmixed material from the container walls.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Moisture in the Slab
          </h2>

          <p>
            The second major cause, and the one specific to concrete countertops rather than sealers generally.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete continues releasing moisture for weeks after it feels dry, and that vapour pushes against a film applied over it. The result is a soft film, often with cloudiness or small blisters, that will not harden until the moisture finds a way out. On a fresh countertop this is extremely common because people underestimate how long concrete takes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twenty-eight days is the usual minimum before sealing a cast concrete countertop, and it is a floor rather than a target. Sealing at two weeks because the slab feels hard and dry is a frequent and expensive error, as our guide on sealing a concrete countertop explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture can also arrive after curing. Washing the slab and sealing the same day, high humidity during application, or a damp workshop all put water into a surface that then gets sealed over. Confirm dryness with a taped square of clear plastic left overnight — condensation underneath means not ready.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diagnostically: moisture problems often show cloudiness, milkiness or blistering alongside the tackiness rather than tackiness alone, and they tend to be worse in the thickest parts of the slab and near the middle rather than at the edges.
          </p>

          <p>
            The prognosis is better than for a mix error. Given warmth, low humidity and time, moisture can work its way out and the film can harden, though a cloudy appearance may persist. For a penetrating sealer the outlook is better still, since there is no continuous film trapping anything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Temperature
          </h2>

          <p>
            Third cause, usually recoverable, and easy to overlook because a room can feel comfortable while the slab is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy cure rate is strongly temperature dependent. Below roughly 15 degrees Celsius many formulations slow dramatically, and below about 10 some will not complete at all, leaving a permanently soft film. Because the reaction generates heat, a cold environment also removes the self-warming that normally helps it along.
          </p>

          <p>
            The temperature that matters is the slab's, not the room's. A concrete countertop is a large thermal mass, and one that has been in a cold garage or workshop can sit well below room temperature for many hours after being brought inside. Measure the slab with an infrared thermometer rather than reading a wall thermostat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based products have an additional failure mode in cold: like water-based paver sealers, their particles must coalesce to form a film, and below the minimum film formation temperature they never fuse, producing a chalky weak layer that warmth afterwards cannot fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diagnostically: a cold-slowed epoxy is usually uniformly soft but firming slowly and will respond to warmth over days. A cold-failed water-based film feels chalky or powdery rather than gummy and will not improve.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warming the room steadily to the product's recommended range and holding it there for several days is the treatment for a cold-slowed epoxy, and it works reasonably often. Do not apply direct heat to the surface, which cures the top while leaving the rest behind.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="contamination" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Contamination and Excess Thickness
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two further causes that produce localised rather than uniform problems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contamination prevents proper curing and adhesion wherever it sits. On a concrete countertop the usual sources are release agent residue from the casting form, polishing slurry not fully removed, silicone from a caulk or a polish, oil from handling, or cleaner residue. Silicone in particular is notorious: minute traces cause fisheyes and soft spots and are very hard to remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            The signature is localised soft or crawling patches in a film that has otherwise cured, often in a pattern that matches where something was handled, wiped or previously treated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excess thickness causes the same skinning-over problem as on any sealed surface: the top cures, the material underneath cannot release its solvent or complete its reaction, and the film stays soft. On a countertop this happens in pooled areas, at edges where product ran and collected, and on the underside of a bullnose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxies in particular have a maximum recommended film thickness per coat, and exceeding it causes both soft curing and, in extreme cases, excessive exotherm — the reaction generating enough heat to discolour or crack the film. Build thickness in multiple thin coats, respecting the recoat window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Diagnostically: thickness problems follow the geography of the slab — low spots, edges, corners, drips on the underside. Contamination follows the geography of use — where hands went, where a rag wiped, where a previous product was applied.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Diagnosis by Pattern</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Uniform</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mix ratio or temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Patchy spots</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Unmixed material or contamination</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Low spots, edges</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Excess thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Cloudy too</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Moisture in the slab</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Fixes Each Cause
          </h2>

          <p>
            The response depends entirely on the diagnosis, which is why the pattern matters so much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix ratio error: removal, and there is no alternative. The chemistry cannot complete, so waiting, warming and recoating all fail. The uncured material must be removed with the appropriate solvent or stripper for that product, the surface cleaned back and prepared, and the sealer reapplied with the ratio measured correctly. Unpleasant, and the only route.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature: warm the room steadily into the product's range and hold it there for several days. This works reasonably often for a cold-slowed epoxy or polyurethane. It does not work for a water-based film that failed to coalesce, which is chalky rather than gummy and needs removal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture: warmth, ventilation, low humidity and patience. It can take one to three weeks. A penetrating sealer will usually come good; a film may harden but retain cloudiness. If the slab was genuinely under-cured, the moisture will keep coming and removal is the honest answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Excess thickness: warmth and time help, and on a badly pooled area, wiping or absorbing surplus while it is still soft can let the remainder cure. Localised removal followed by a careful recoat is often practical because the problem is localised.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contamination: local removal, thorough cleaning to eliminate the contaminant, and recoating. If silicone is involved, expect to work hard at it, because trace silicone spreads and persists.
          </p>

          <p>
            What not to do in any case: do not apply another coat over an uncured film, which traps the problem permanently. Do not sand a soft film, which clogs abrasive and embeds debris. And do not wash it, which adds moisture to at least one of the likely causes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Preventing a Repeat
          </h2>

          <p>
            The redo is the opportunity to eliminate every cause above, and it is worth the extra hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weigh two-part products on a digital scale if the manufacturer specifies by weight, and use accurate graduated containers if by volume. Do not estimate, do not eyeball a line on a cup, and do not assume the two methods are interchangeable — they are not, because the components have different densities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the double-pot method. Mix thoroughly in the first container, scraping sides and bottom, then transfer to a clean second container and mix again. This eliminates unmixed material from the walls, which is the single most common source of localised sticky patches.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm the slab is dry with a taped plastic square overnight, and confirm it is at least 28 days old if freshly cast. Check the slab temperature with an infrared thermometer rather than the room thermostat, because a large concrete mass lags room temperature by hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean meticulously and avoid contaminants. No silicone-containing products anywhere near the surface, remove all polishing residue, degrease, and handle the cleaned slab with gloves. Our guide on sealing a concrete countertop covers preparation in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply thin coats within the product's maximum film thickness, and mix batches you can apply inside the pot life. Building the film in several thin passes is both a better finish and a direct prevention of the thickness failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            And do a test piece. A spare offcut of the same concrete, sealed with the same batch under the same conditions, tells you within a day whether the mix and the conditions are right — before the same material goes onto the countertop you are going to live with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring a Two-Part Product by Volume When It Specifies Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The two ratios differ because resin and hardener have different densities, so measuring the wrong way produces an off-ratio mix that can never fully cure. Extra hardener is as damaging as extra resin. Use a digital scale when the manufacturer specifies by weight.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Scraping the Mixing Container</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unmixed resin clinging to the sides and bottom goes onto the countertop as patches that stay sticky permanently. Mix thoroughly scraping the walls, then transfer to a clean second container and mix again. This double-pot method exists specifically to eliminate this failure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting for an Off-Ratio Epoxy to Cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mis-measured two-part product has surplus of one component with nothing to react with, and that stays in the film permanently. Warmth, time and recoating all fail. Recognising this early saves weeks of hoping before you accept that removal is the only route.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a Countertop Less Than 28 Days Old</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete releases moisture for weeks after it feels hard and dry, and a film over it traps that vapour, producing a soft, cloudy, sometimes blistered surface. Twenty-eight days is a minimum rather than a target, and a taped plastic square left overnight confirms readiness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Another Coat to Fix a Tacky One</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This traps the uncured material permanently under a new film and turns a problem that might have resolved into one that definitely requires removal. It is the most common instinct and the most reliably damaging response on a countertop.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my concrete countertop sealer still sticky?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a two-part product, most often an incorrect mix ratio or inadequate mixing, which never cures regardless of time. Otherwise: moisture still coming out of the slab, a slab or room too cold, excess film thickness, or contamination such as silicone or release agent residue on the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will off-ratio epoxy eventually harden?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The reaction is stoichiometric, so surplus resin or hardener has nothing to react with and remains in the film permanently as an uncured fraction. Warming it, waiting longer and coating over it all fail. The material has to be removed and the surface resealed with a correctly measured mix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should epoxy countertop sealer take to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Touch dry in four to twelve hours, hard in 24 to 72, and full chemical cure in seven to 30 days depending on the product. An epoxy still slightly soft at 24 hours is normal; one that is liquid, gummy or fingerprinting at 72 hours in a warm room has a problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can cold weather stop countertop sealer curing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Below about 15 degrees Celsius many epoxies slow dramatically and below 10 some will not complete at all. The temperature that matters is the slab's rather than the room's, since a concrete mass lags room temperature by hours. Warming the room steadily over several days often rescues a cold-slowed epoxy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if it is the mix or the moisture?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at the pattern. Uniform tackiness across the whole surface on a two-part product points at the mix. Tackiness with cloudiness, milkiness or small blisters, worse in the middle and thickest parts, points at moisture from the slab. Localised sticky spots suggest unmixed material or contamination.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I sand a tacky countertop sealer smooth?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Abrasive clogs immediately on an uncured film and you embed debris into the soft material, making removal harder. Diagnose the cause first: if it is mix ratio, remove it; if it is cold or moisture, warmth and time may still resolve it without any mechanical intervention.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes fisheyes or crawling in countertop sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Contamination, most often silicone, which spreads in trace amounts and is very persistent. Other sources are form release agent, polishing slurry, oil from handling and cleaner residue. It shows as localised crawling or soft spots in a film that has otherwise cured, in a pattern matching where something was handled or wiped.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

